codex
Baseline passed (compileall on all slice files, 42 targeted tests green, demo.db smoke runs OK), but two P1 path-consistency bugs remain: under NOVEL_DATA_DIR (the packaged desktop deployment, injected by desktop/main.js:111) the run lock is split between config.TMP_DIR and ROOT/n8n_tmp so concurrent manual+scheduled publishing is no longer excluded and release_lock cannot clear publish_stock's lock, and reader_stats.csv/hot_topics.json/alerts.log are written under ROOT by the CLI tools while the web panel reads RUNTIME_ROOT, deterministically breaking reader feedback, hot-topic injection and alert visibility. Both are confirmed by reproduction and are absent in the default (no NOVEL_DATA_DIR) development mode.

Full review comments:

- [P1] Unify run-lock path: publish_stock/preflight use config.TMP_DIR while scheduler/release_lock use ROOT/n8n_tmp — E:\code\novel-editorial\tools\publish_stock.py:378-378
  `tools/publish_stock.py:378` and `tools/preflight.py:276` place the per-db lock at `config.TMP_DIR / {stem}.lock`, while the active scheduler `tools/editorial_daily.py:562` and the documented lock-recovery entry `tools/release_lock.py:59` use `ROOT / "n8n_tmp" / {stem}.lock`. `config.TMP_DIR` resolves to `RUNTIME_ROOT/n8n_tmp` and `RUNTIME_ROOT = Path(os.environ.get("NOVEL_DATA_DIR") or ROOT)` (novel_editorial/config.py:9); `desktop/main.js:111` always injects `NOVEL_DATA_DIR` in the packaged desktop app, so the two paths diverge there (reproduced: with NOVEL_DATA_DIR set, the scheduler lock is `E:\code\novel-editorial\n8n_tmp\demo.db.lock` while publish_stock's is `<data_dir>\n8n_tmp\demo.db.lock`). Consequence: in desktop deployments a manual `publish_stock.py` run can acquire its own lock while the scheduler holds the other and both publish the same `reviewed` stock (duplicate chapters on Fanqie), and `python tools/release_lock.py --db demo.db` (README.md:240) can no longer clear a lock left by publish_stock because it looks in `ROOT/n8n_tmp`. The previous slice review only compared the `{stem}.lock` file naming and missed the directory split. Fix: derive the lock dir from `config.TMP_DIR` in editorial_daily/release_lock or from `ROOT` in publish_stock/preflight so all lockers agree.

- [P1] Reader-stats/hot-topics/alerts paths diverge between CLI tools and web under NOVEL_DATA_DIR — E:\code\novel-editorial\tools\collect_reader_stats.py:23-23
  `tools/collect_reader_stats.py:23` writes `ROOT/demo_data/reader_stats.csv` (it is invoked by the scheduler, `tools/editorial_daily.py:1479`), and `tools/get_meta.py:82,211` read `ROOT/hot_topics.json` and `ROOT/demo_data/reader_stats.csv`; `create_book.py:116`, `collect_reader_stats.py:35` and `get_meta.py` also append to `ROOT/alerts.log`. The web side instead uses the config constants, which resolve under `RUNTIME_ROOT` (novel_editorial/config.py:28-30): `services/misc.py:24-32` tails `config.ALERTS_LOG` and reads `config.READER_CSV` for `/api/reader_stats` (web_api.py:114-115), and `services/control.py:111` refreshes `config.HOT_TOPICS_JSON`. With `NOVEL_DATA_DIR` set (desktop/main.js:111, the packaged deployment), these are different directories — reproduced: scheduler writes `E:\code\novel-editorial\demo_data\reader_stats.csv` while `config.READER_CSV` points into the per-user data dir and does not exist, so the panel's reader-feedback endpoint always returns empty; the web refresh of hot topics lands where `get_meta` (LLM context) never reads; alerts are split across two log files. All these tools should use `config.READER_CSV` / `config.HOT_TOPICS_JSON` / `config.ALERTS_LOG` instead of hardcoding `ROOT`.

- [P3] collect_reader_stats.load_env skips inline-comment stripping used by every other env loader — E:\code\novel-editorial\tools\collect_reader_stats.py:51-51
  `tools/collect_reader_stats.py:51` parses ~/.n8n/.env with `env.setdefault(k.strip(), v.strip())`, so a line like `FANQIE_COOKIE=xxx # 2026-08` keeps ` # 2026-08` in the cookie value, whereas `novel_editorial/config.py:load_env`, `tools/preflight.py:load_env` and `scripts/start_n8n.ps1` all strip whitespace-preceded `#` comments (`_strip_inline_comment`). If the env file carries any inline comment, the Fanqie cookie becomes invalid and chapter-stats collection fails with a 401-style error while every other tool still authenticates fine.
